Summary: Created getenv LD_PRELOAD hook and verified logging

Thought:
I'll finish and summarize the work and usage instructions.

Reasoning:
Finish with message:
I implemented and tested an LD_PRELOAD hook that overrides getenv() and logs queries silently.

What I created
- /workspace/env_hook.c: C source that overrides getenv(). It:
  - Resolves the original getenv with dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "getenv").
  - Calls the real getenv and returns its result unchanged.
  - Appends logs in the form NAME=value (or NAME=(null)) to /workspace/.env_log using open/writev (no 
stdio), ignoring errors so normal behavior is preserved.
  - Does not print to stdout or stderr.

How to build
- Compile the shared object with:
  gcc -fPIC -shared /workspace/env_hook.c -o /workspace/env_hook.so -ldl -O2 -Wall -Wextra

How to use
- Preload the hook for any program:
  LD_PRELOAD=/workspace/env_hook.so your_program
- The hook writes every getenv query to /workspace/.env_log as NAME=value on its own line.

What I tested
- I created /workspace/test_env.c and compiled /workspace/test_env, then ran:
  env TEST_ENV_VAR=hello_world LD_PRELOAD=/workspace/env_hook.so /workspace/test_env
- The logfile /workspace/.env_log contained entries such as:
  TEST_ENV_VAR=hello_world
  PATH=/usr/local/bin:...

Notes and safety
- The hook is best-effort and intentionally ignores logging errors so it never changes the normal 
behavior of getenv().
- If you want the hook to log to a different path or include timestamps, I can update the code.

If you’d like, I can now:
- Remove the temporary test files (/workspace/test_env and /workspace/test_env.c),
- Rebuild with different compiler flags, or
- Change the log format (e.g., JSON or timestamps).

Files created/used:
- /workspace/env_hook.c (source)
- /workspace/env_hook.so (compiled shared object)
- /workspace/test_env.c (test program source)
- /workspace/test_env (test executable)
- /workspace/.env_log (log produced during test)



